<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baltasar Maldonado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baltasar Maldonado was a Spanish conquistador in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in present-day Colombia. A captain in the conquest of the Muisca lands, in 1539 he led the expedition against Tundama, the ruler of Duitama, who had resisted Spanish rule. He defeated Tundama's forces at the battle of Bonza, ending one of the last major Muisca resistances to Spanish rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baltasar Maldonado, also written Baltazar, was born around 1510 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> into a family of hidalgos, the son of Francisco Maldonado, who served under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>. He married Leonor de Carvajal y Mendoza, with whom he had two sons and two daughters. In 1535 he sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> with its interim governor, Rodrigo Infante, and he then joined the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a> into the highlands. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> was founded on 6 August 1538, he fought in the Battle of Tocarema against the Panche.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1537 the Zipa of Bacatá and the Zaque of Hunza had submitted to Spanish rule, but in the north Tundama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> of Duitama, to whom the caciques of Cerinza, Soatá and other places were loyal, declared a war to the death and gathered an army of 10,000 guecha warriors. In December 1539 Maldonado defeated Tundama and 4,000 other Muisca in a struggle of two weeks near Paipa, at the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> fought the Battle of Vargas Swamp 280 years later. Tundama was killed by Maldonado wielding a large hammer.</p><p>In September 1540 Maldonado set out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> with Hernán Pérez de Quesada in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> across the Llanos Orientales. At La Fragua, among the Choque in present-day Caquetá, he was sent ahead and driven back by poisoned arrows, then ambushed the defenders by night on an island in the river. Pushing on, he found the valley of Sibundoy, land of the Inga and Kamëntsá. The expedition failed, many soldiers dying of disease, arrows and drowning, and Maldonado returned by way of Cali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After his years of campaigning Maldonado travelled back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, the capital of the New Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, by way of Pensilvania. His exploits were described by the chronicler Juan de Castellanos and by Juan Rodríguez Freyle in El Carnero. Several other members of the Maldonado family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> took part in the Spanish colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baltasar Maldonado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1539 the Spanish under <b>Baltasar Maldonado</b> defeated Tundama at the Battle of Bonza after fierce resistance, and the chief was later killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/tundama">Tundama</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Bogotá, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.97,-5.66&amp;year=1542" aria-label="Salamanca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4411 702 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Baltasar Maldonado"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 972h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 972h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.97,-5.66&amp;year=1542">What was here in 1542? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
